        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br> 
This investigation was designed to assess muscle sympathetic nerve activity and its role in orthostatic intolerance (OI) through measurements of the vestibulosympathetic reflex. Microgravity has been shown to elicit marked changes in the vestibular system. A number of countermeasures have been developed to prevent or alleviate post-spaceflight OI. However, as yet, no single countermeasure has effectively prevented post-spaceflight OI. 
<p>
Data from this campaign were used to determine if the vestibulosympathetic reflex is altered during and after bed rest and if ultrasonic bone stimulation (UBS) of the mastoid improved orthostatic tolerance and thus could serve as an effective and simple countermeasure for post-spaceflight orthostatic intolerance (OI). 
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
The microneurographic method was used for measurement of sympathetic nerve activity. Cardiac parameters measured included arterial pressure, heart rate, blood flow, mean blood velocity measured by Doppler ultrasound, arterial diameter, vascular resistance and conductance. Respiration pattern data also were collected in these subjects. Pitch head rotation was used to stimulate the vestibular system. 
<p>
The head-up tilt protocol was used to stimulate orthostatic intolerance. UBS of the mastoid bone was used to determine if orthostatic tolerance was improved. 
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Specific Aim 1: To study the effects of prolonged bed rest on the vestibulosympathetic reflex (VSR).<BR> Result: The hypothesis that the VSR would be attenuated after approximately 35 days of head-down bed rest (HDBR) was tested. MSNA and leg blood flow were measured before and after 36±1 days of HDBR in 7 subjects. The results from this study demonstrate a significant attenuation of the VSR after prolonged HDBR. These data provide support for the concept that attenuation of the VSR could contribute to an inadequate increase in MSNA observed post-HDBR in those subjects that experience orthostatic intolerance.
<P>
Specific Aim 2: To determine the effect of ultrasonic mastoid stimulation on orthostatic tolerance following one-day HDBR. <BR>Result: A head-up tilt test was conducted on 25 subjects after one-day HDBR with and without ultrasonic bone stimulation (UBS) (HiSonic®). Twelve out of the 25 subjects showed an increase in tilt time using UBS. The limitation of this study was that tilt was limited to 30 minutes and that one-day bed rest did not induce presyncopal symptoms in all subjects. These initial studies do show that UBS may improve orthostatic intolerance following bed rest.
<P>
Specific Aim 3: To determine the effect of ultrasonic mastoid stimulation on MSNA.<BR> Results: To test the effect of artificial activation of the otolith organs, UBS was applied to the mastoid bone and the clavicle and compared to head down rotation. The results from this study demonstrate that UBS can produce increased sympathetic outflow similar to that seen during natural stimulation.
<P>
Specific Aim 4: To test the hypothesis that the VSR modulation of MSNA would be unaffected by baroreceptor unloading and would be inhibited during baroreceptor loading. <BR>Results: The findings from the study supported the hypothesis.
<P>
Specific Aim 5: To test the influence of dramamine (motion sickness drug) on sympathetic neural responses to orthostatic stress and vestibular activation in humans.<BR> Result: Dramamine augments the cardiac-vagal baroreflex, but does not alter resting MSNA, the sympathetic baroreflex, or the VSR.
<P>
Specific Aim 6: To test the hypothesis that endurance training reduces renal vasoconstriction to orthostatic stress.<BR> Result: The findings from the study supported the hypothesis.
<P>
Specific Aim 7: To test the hemodynamic effects of mental stress during orthostatic stress.<BR> Result: Augmented sympathetic activity of the upright posture does not alter vascular responses of the visceral organs or forearm to MS.
<P>
Specific Aim 8: To test the effect of aldosterone on the baroreflex in humans. <BR>Result: Aldosterone impairs the arterial baroreflexes in humans.<P>
This experiment has concluded in ground-based studies sponsored by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration's Human Adaptation and Countermeasures Division. This experiment was conducted during Bed Rest Campaigns 3A and 3B. LSDA doesn't expect to receive results from this study.<END>
